1. **An axial magnetic gear comprising:**

a first discoid rotor and a second discoid rotor disposed about a common rotational axis and axially spaced from one another, each discoid rotor including a circumferential array of magnetic poles;

an intermediate linking rotor positioned axially between the first and second discoid rotors and rotatable about the common rotational axis, the intermediate linking rotor including a first circumferential array of magnetic poles facing the first discoid rotor and a second circumferential array of magnetic poles facing the second discoid rotor, the first and second circumferential arrays being mechanically linked for conjoint rotation;

a first magnetic-field-modulating yoke positioned in a first axial air gap between the first discoid rotor and the intermediate linking rotor; and

a second magnetic-field-modulating yoke positioned in a second axial air gap between the second discoid rotor and the intermediate linking rotor,

wherein each magnetic-field-modulating yoke comprises a circumferential series of ferromagnetic flux-modulating portions separated by magnetically resistive portions and is configured to modulate a magnetic field exchanged between the intermediate linking rotor and the respective discoid rotor, such that rotation of at least one of the discoid rotors produces torque on the intermediate linking rotor through a non-contact axial magnetic-gearing interaction.

1. **A magnetic rotary apparatus comprising:**  

   a first disk-shaped magnetic rotor rotatable about a rotational axis;  

   a second disk-shaped magnetic rotor coaxial with and axially spaced from the first disk-shaped magnetic rotor;  

   a first circumferential array of magnetic bodies positioned to modulate a magnetic field produced by the first disk-shaped magnetic rotor;  

   a second circumferential array of magnetic bodies positioned to modulate a magnetic field produced by the second disk-shaped magnetic rotor; and  

   a disk-shaped linking rotor disposed axially between the first disk-shaped magnetic rotor and the second disk-shaped magnetic rotor and magnetically coupled thereto.

An illustrative apparatus claim could read:

> **1. A dual-stage discoid magnetic gear, comprising:**  
> a first magnetic rotor rotatable about a rotational axis;  
> a second magnetic rotor rotatable about the rotational axis and axially spaced from the first magnetic rotor;  
> a central magnetic linking rotor disposed axially between the first magnetic rotor and the second magnetic rotor and rotatable about the rotational axis, the central magnetic linking rotor having a first magnetic structure facing the first magnetic rotor and a second magnetic structure facing the second magnetic rotor;  
> a first field-modulating yoke disposed in a first axial air gap between the first magnetic rotor and the central magnetic linking rotor, the first field-modulating yoke comprising a circumferential array of first ferromagnetic flux-modulating elements configured to modulate a magnetic field exchanged between the first magnetic rotor and the first magnetic structure to form a first magnetic-gear stage; and  
> a second field-modulating yoke disposed in a second axial air gap between the central magnetic linking rotor and the second magnetic rotor, the second field-modulating yoke comprising a circumferential array of second ferromagnetic flux-modulating elements configured to modulate a magnetic field exchanged between the second magnetic structure and the second magnetic rotor to form a second magnetic-gear stage,  
> **wherein** the central magnetic linking rotor magnetically couples the first magnetic-gear stage to the second magnetic-gear stage such that torque applied to one of the first magnetic rotor, the second magnetic rotor, or the central magnetic linking rotor produces rotation of at least one other thereof at a speed determined by respective pole counts of the magnetic structures and respective numbers of the flux-modulating elements.

Depending on the intended novelty, the claim could be narrowed by specifying that the two yokes are stationary or independently rotatable, that the linking rotor is a unitary rotor, the relative pole-count relationships, opposite-face permanent-magnet arrays, or a compound gear ratio. This is an illustrative drafting example, not a patentability or legal opinion.
